  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Increasing access, retention and performance in primary education (Sierra Leone)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Studies have shown that children who participate in pre-school are more likely to enrol, and achieve better results in primary school. In recent years only two in three children completed primary level education in Sierra Leone. Overall the quality of education is poor, with no early childhood development provision. Educational attainment of girls is particularly low, in part due to gender-based violence, high levels of teenage pregnancy and harmful cultural practices, such as early marriage. This project will introduce a community-based model of early childhood development and prepare pre-schoolers to enter primary school. The project will also deliver life skills education to older children on topics including child protection, sex education and teenage pregnancy. It will support teachers to improve classroom teach and learning practice. As a result enrolment, retention and performance in primary schools will be enhanced; communities will understand the importance of early childhood development and actively support their children, including the girls, through their primary education; teachers will be better able to engage with and motivate the children; and a cadre of &#x2018;master trainers&apos; will become experts on implementing community-based early childhood development and life skills education.</narrative>
  </description>
